00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:03.370
Welcome to AI Agentic Design Patterns

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.573
with AutoGen, built in partnership
with Microsoft.

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.344
I'm joined by the instructors Chi Wang,
who is principal researcher at Microsoft,

4
00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:15.015
and Qingyun Wu, who's an assistant professor
at Penn State University,

5
00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:18.251
both of whom are co-creators of AutoGen.

6
00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:21.221
Thanks, Andrew. I'm excited to be here.

7
00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.623
Thank you.
Great to be here with you, Andrew.

8
00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:26.192
This course is an introduction
to AutoGen,

9
00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.429
a multi-agent conversational framework
that enables you

10
00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.665
to quickly create multiple agents
with different roles,

11
00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:36.970
persona tasks, and capabilities
to implement complex AI applications

12
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.973
using different AI agentic design patterns.

13
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.977
Let's say you're interested in analyzing
financial data.

14
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.715
The task may require writing
code to collect and analyze share prices.

15
00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:51.718
Then synthesizing
your findings into a report.

16
00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:55.355
This might take a person days of research
and coding and writing.

17
00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:58.425
A multi-agent system can streamline

18
00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:01.928
this process
by enabling you to create and hire

19
00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:07.033
agents that work for you as a researcher,
data collector, co-writer, and executor.

20
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.703
Your agents can also
iteratively review, critique,

21
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:13.706
and improve the results
until it meets your standard.

22
00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:14.908
This is just one example

23
00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:18.645
of the many practical applications
of a multi-agent framework.

24
00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:22.449
And Chi and Qingyun will guide you through
six lessons,

25
00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:26.119
each featuring its own unique
design process and use case.

26
00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:29.789
In this course, you'll learn about how
to train core components

27
00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:33.226
by exploring the building
agent conversable agent.

28
00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:38.665
You'll create and customize
a lively conversation between two standup

29
00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:43.665
comedian agents while exploring
their interactive capabilities.

30
00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:45.205
Then you will learn

31
00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:48.741
about a multi-agent interaction
pattern called Sequential chats.

32
00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:53.213
This allows you to build
conversational agents that work

33
00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:56.916
step by step to carry out a list of tasks
in a sequence.

34
00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:01.221
We'll illustrate this
with a customer onboarding application.

35
00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:06.654
You will also explore the agent reflection
framework in two practical scenarios.

36
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.996
The first one will use multiple agents

37
00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:12.966
to produce a well-written blogpost,

38
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.768
the second as tools to agents

39
00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:18.571
to create a conversational chess game.

40
00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.940
In both of these examples, you will learn

41
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.210
another interaction
pattern called a nested chat,

42
00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.981
which means when the agent is
given a task,

43
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.050
it calls a bunch of other agents
and iterates

44
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.687
with them for a while
before it returns the result.

45
00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:37.056
If we think of you,

46
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:40.326
the developer,
as a manager of a handful of agents,

47
00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:44.297
then nested chat corresponds
to letting an agent

48
00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:49.631
that you manage also in turn, manage
their own agents. You also learn about

49
00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:55.069
a very powerful capability tool use. In which
you can provide a user-defined function.

50
00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:59.312
For example, a function
to check of certain chess moves are legal

51
00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:02.482
and give that to the agent to use.

52
00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:04.884
And for applications
where you might not have

53
00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:08.421
a predefined function
or predefined piece of code to provide,

54
00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:13.721
you'll also learn about coding and code
execution, which means asking the agent

55
00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:18.031
to write the code it needs,
which after checking for correctness,

56
00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:19.832
it could then execute that code

57
00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:23.136
it did wrote.
Maybe in a sandbox environment to computer

58
00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:27.440
result needed for a task.
You'll see both tool use as well as code

59
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.944
writing and execution illustrated
in the financial analysis example.

60
00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.946
And finally you learn best

61
00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:36.482
practices for
building custom multi-agent group chats.

62
00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:41.254
We'll illustrate this with examples
of generating a detailed report.

63
00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:44.691
This complex task requires planning.

64
00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:49.295
In other words, the agent has to decide
on a sequence of actions to take,

65
00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:54.133
such as guiding data,
analyzing, writing, and revising.

66
00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:59.634
You see how we can add a planning agent
into the group chat and control

67
00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:03.176
how the work flows from one agent
to another agent,

68
00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:07.847
so as to execute the needed individual
tasks in the right sequence.

69
00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:10.783
Many people have worked
to create this course.

70
00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:15.121
I'd like to thank Eric Zhu from Microsoft.
As well as Diala Ezzeddine

71
00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.090
from DeepLearning.AI.

72
00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.027
In this first lesson, you will start
with building your first AutoGen

73
00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:24.430
agent. Program
a basic two-agent conversation,

74
00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:27.300
and enjoy a standup comedy show.

75
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.334
That sounds great.

76
00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.603
Let's go on to the
next video and get started.

